{"name":"French Blue","hex":"#0072bb","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"french","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To prepare food by cutting it into strips.","To kiss (another person) while inserting one’s tongue into the other person's mouth."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/french"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bleu de France (colour)","blocks":[{"text":"Bleu de France is a colour traditionally used to represent France. Blue has been used in the heraldry of the French monarchy since at least the 12th century, with the golden fleurs-de-lis of the kings always set on a blue background. A brighter version, based on the blue of the French Tricolour, is used in modern times, particularly in a sporting context. French national teams in all sports will normally use blue as their main colour."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bleu_de_France_(colour)"},{"title":"French Bee","blocks":[{"text":"French Bee SAS, styled French bee, and formerly named French Blue, is a French low-cost, long-haul airline based at Paris Orly Airport. It operates a scheduled network between France and worldwide leisure destinations with a fleet of Airbus A350 aircraft. Its headquarters are inside the parent company Groupe Dubreuil's offices in the Belleville-sur-Vie area of Bellevigny, Vendée, France."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/French_bee%2C_F-HMIB%2C_Airbus_A350-1041_%2854010557417%29.jpg/330px-French_bee%2C_F-HMIB%2C_Airbus_A350-1041_%2854010557417%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_Bee"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Bugatti_Typ_35C_Grand_Prix_Racer_1926.jpg/120px-Bugatti_Typ_35C_Grand_Prix_Racer_1926.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Bugatti_Typ_35C_Grand_Prix_Racer_1926.jpg/250px-Bugatti_Typ_35C_Grand_Prix_Racer_1926.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Bugatti_Typ_35C_Grand_Prix_Racer_1926.jpg/120px-Bugatti_Typ_35C_Grand_Prix_Racer_1926.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABugatti_Typ_35C_Grand_Prix_Racer_1926.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Delage_D6.jpg/120px-Delage_D6.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedi